/*
Simple intuition

Addition of two bits:

Sum bit = a_bit ^ b_bit ^ carry

Carry = 1 if at least two of the three bits are 1

Build result bit by bit from least significant bit (LSB) to most significant (MSB).

After 32 bits, check the sign bit:

If set → result is negative → convert to proper two’s complement.


Time complexity

O(32) = O(1) → fixed 32-bit integer

Space complexity

O(1) → only a few ints (res, carry, a_bit, b_bit)

Step-by-step mental model

Loop 32 times: process each bit of a and b

XOR handles sum, AND/logic handles carry

Shift bits into place using res |= (cur << i)

At the end:

If MSB (sign bit) is 1, convert raw 32-bit pattern to negative using two’s complement:

res = ~res + 1;

Why XOR for sum, not OR

XOR = “1 if odd number of bits 1” → matches binary sum

OR = “1 if at least 1 bit 1” → would overcount sum, break addition

Mask / negative conversion
if(res > 0x7FFFFFFF){
    res = ~res + 1; // convert to negative two's complement
}


0x7FFFFFFF → largest positive int

If res exceeds this, sign bit is 1 → interpret correctly as negative

Mental trigger

“Bitwise addition = XOR for sum, AND/logic for carry, handle sign at the end.”
*/
class Solution {
    public int getSum(int a, int b) {
        int carry = 0 , res = 0;
        int mask = 0xFFFFFFFF;
        for(int i=0;i<32;i++){
            int a_bit = (a>>i) & 1;
            int b_bit = (b>>i) & 1;
            int cur = a_bit ^ b_bit ^ carry;
            carry = (a_bit + b_bit + carry) >=2 ?1:0;
            if(cur !=0){
                res|= (1<<i);
            }
        }
        if(res > 0x7FFFFFFF){
            res = ~ (res);
            res += 1;
        }
        return res;
    }
}
